            "summary": "Tethys Geosyncline, breakup of Gondwanaland, northward collision of the Indian Plate with the Eurasian Plate in the Eocene (~50 mya), ongoing convergence (~5 cm/year), young fold mountain characteristics, and syntaxial bends (Nanga Parbat and Namcha Barwa).",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<p><strong>Geotectonic Origin of the Himalayas:</strong>\nThe <strong>Himalayas</strong> represent the loftiest, youngest, and most tectonically active <strong>Fold Mountain</strong> system on Earth, spanning an arcuate distance of approximately <strong>$2,400\\text{ to }2,500\\text{ km}$</strong> across northern India, Nepal, Bhutan, Pakistan, and China:</p>\n<p><strong>Plate Tectonic Mechanism (Continental Collision):</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The Tethys Geosyncline:</strong>\n  During the Mesozoic Era (approx. 200 million years ago), the ancient supercontinent <strong>Pangaea</strong> split into two super-landmasses: <strong>Laurasia (Angaraland)</strong> in the north and <strong>Gondwanaland</strong> in the south. Between these two landmasses lay a long, narrow, shallow marine trough termed the <strong>Tethys Sea (Tethys Geosyncline)</strong>. For millions of years, rivers draining Angaraland and Gondwanaland deposited colossal volumes of sediments into this geosynclinal basin.</li>\n<li><strong>The Northward Drift & Collision:</strong>\n  Around 100 million years ago, the <strong>Indian Plate</strong> broke away from Gondwanaland (near Madagascar/Antarctica) and began a rapid northward drift at rates exceeding $12–15\\text{ cm/year}$ across the Neo-Tethys Ocean.\n  Around <strong>$50\\text{ to }55\\text{ million years ago}$ (Early Eocene epoch)</strong>, the northern continental margin of the Indian Plate collided with the southern continental margin of the <strong>Eurasian Plate</strong> (continent-continent collision).</li>\n<li><strong>Orogenesis in Three Successive Phases:</strong>\n  Because both continental lithospheres were buoyant and could not subduct deeply, the intervening marine sediments of the Tethys Geosyncline were compressed, folded, faulted, and uplifted in three major tectonic episodes:\n  <ol>\n    <li><strong>First Phase (Oligocene, ~35 mya):</strong> Uplift of the <strong>Greater Himalayas (Himadri)</strong>.</li>\n    <li><strong>Second Phase (Miocene, ~20–15 mya):</strong> Uplift of the <strong>Lesser Himalayas (Himachal / Middle Himalayas)</strong>.</li>\n    <li><strong>Third Phase (Pliocene to Pleistocene, ~5–1.5 mya):</strong> Uplift of the outermost <strong>Shiwalik Range</strong> and formation of the foredeep that became the Indo-Gangetic Plains.</li>\n  </ol>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Ongoing Tectonic Convergence:</strong>\n  The Indian Plate continues to push northward beneath the Eurasian Plate at a rate of approximately <strong>$4\\text{ to }5\\text{ cm per year}$</strong>, resulting in ongoing crustal shortening, periodic high-magnitude earthquakes, and an uplift of the Himalayan peaks by approximately $5\\text{ to }10\\text{ mm per year}$.</li>\n</ul>\n</p>\n<p><strong>The Syntaxial Bends (Hairpin Bends) of the Himalayas:</strong>\nThe general strike of the Himalayas is West-Northwest to East-Southeast. However, at its eastern and western extremities, the Himalayan chain abruptly bends southward in sharp knee-like or hairpin turns termed <strong>Syntaxial Bends</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Western Syntaxial Bend:</strong> Occurs in the west near <strong>Nanga Parbat ($8,126\\text{ m}$)</strong> in northern Pakistan/PoK, where the Indus River cuts an extraordinary $5,200\\text{-meter}$ deep gorge. Here, the ranges bend sharply south into the Suleiman and Kirthar ranges.</li>\n<li><strong>Eastern Syntaxial Bend:</strong> Occurs in the east near <strong>Namcha Barwa ($7,782\\text{ m}$)</strong> in Tibet/Arunachal Pradesh, where the Brahmaputra (Tsangpo) River carves a colossal hairpin canyon (the Great Bend of Yarlung Tsangpo) and slices through into India, sweeping the ranges southward into the <strong>Purvanchal</strong> (Patkai, Naga, and Arakan Yoma).</li>\n<li><em>Cause of Syntaxial Bends:</em> Caused by the rigid resistance offered by the north-projecting promontories of the underlying Indian Peninsular Shield: the <strong>Kashmir/Delhi promontory</strong> in the northwest and the <strong>Assam/Meghalaya promontory</strong> in the northeast.</li>\n</ul>\n</p>\n",
