            "theoryHtml": "\n<p><strong>Longitudinal Morpho-Structural Zones:</strong>\nFrom north to south, the Himalayan collision zone is structured into four distinct parallel geological and tectonic subdivisions, separated from one another by deep-seated regional thrust fault zones:</p>\n<p>\n<table style=\"width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; margin-top:8px; margin-bottom:12px;\" border=\"1\">\n<tr style=\"background:#f4f4f4;\">\n<th style=\"padding:6px;\">Zone Number (North to South)</th>\n<th style=\"padding:6px;\">Longitudinal Morphological Zone</th>\n<th style=\"padding:6px;\">Average Elevation & Width</th>\n<th style=\"padding:6px;\">Bounding Tectonic Faults (Thrust Boundaries)</th>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\">Zone 1</td>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\"><strong>Trans-Himalayas (Tibetan Himalayas)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\">Elevation: $4,500–5,500\\text{ m}$; Width: $\\sim 40\\text{ km}$</td>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\">Separated from the Greater Himalayas by the <strong>Indus-Tsangpo Suture Zone (ITSZ)</strong>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\">Zone 2</td>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\"><strong>Greater Himalayas (Himadri / Inner Himalayas)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\">Elevation: $\\sim 6,100\\text{ m}$; Width: $120–190\\text{ km}$</td>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\">Separated from the Lesser Himalayas by the <strong>Main Central Thrust (MCT)</strong>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\">Zone 3</td>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\"><strong>Lesser Himalayas (Himachal / Middle Himalayas)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\">Elevation: $3,700–4,500\\text{ m}$; Width: $60–80\\text{ km}$</td>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\">Separated from the Shiwalik Range by the <strong>Main Boundary Thrust (MBT)</strong>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\">Zone 4</td>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\"><strong>Outer Himalayas (Shiwalik Range / Sub-Himalayas)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\">Elevation: $900–1,200\\text{ m}$; Width: $10–50\\text{ km}$</td>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\">Separated from the Indo-Gangetic Alluvial Plain by the <strong>Himalayan Frontal Fault (HFF)</strong>.</td>\n</tr>\n</table>\n</p>\n<p><strong>The Four Great Boundary Thrust Faults:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>1. Indus-Tsangpo Suture Zone (ITSZ) / Yarlung-Zangbo Suture:</strong>\n  <ul>\n    <li>The exact line of collision where the Indian Plate welded with the Eurasian Plate.</li>\n    <li>Marked by <strong>Ophiolites</strong> (fragments of ancient ocean crust and upper mantle obducted onto land), radiolarian cherts, and volcanic melanges.</li>\n    <li>The Indus River flows along this structural depression in the west, and the Tsangpo flows along it in the east.</li>\n  </ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>2. Main Central Thrust (MCT):</strong>\n  <ul>\n    <li>A massive north-dipping ductile shear zone separating the high-grade metamorphic crystalline rocks (granites, gneisses, schists) of the <strong>Great Himalayas</strong> from the lower-grade metasedimentary rocks of the <strong>Lesser Himalayas</strong>.</li>\n    <li>Extends continuously along the entire length of the Himalayan arc.</li>\n  </ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>3. Main Boundary Thrust (MBT):</strong>\n  <ul>\n    <li>A prominent regional thrust fault separating the pre-Tertiary rocks of the <strong>Lesser Himalayas</strong> from the late Tertiary molasse sediments of the <strong>Shiwalik Range</strong>.</li>\n    <li>Highly active neotectonically, with significant earthquake focal epicenters.</li>\n  </ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>4. Himalayan Frontal Fault (HFF) / Main Frontal Thrust (MFT):</strong>\n  <ul>\n    <li>The southernmost and youngest tectonic boundary fault of the Himalayan system.</li>\n    <li>Separates the front of the rising <strong>Shiwalik foothills</strong> from the flat alluvial sediments of the <strong>Indo-Gangetic Plains</strong>.</li>\n    <li>Under active north-south compression; directly responsible for ongoing seismic deformation and the lifting of river terraces in the Bhabar zone.</li>\n  </ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</p>\n",
