            "summary": "Youngest mountain range (Pliocene-Pleistocene), elevation 900–1,200 m, unconsolidated molasse gravels, longitudinal valleys called 'Duns' in the west and 'Duars' in the east, regional names (Jammu Hills, Churia Ghat), and seasonal Chos torrents.",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<p><strong>The Outer Himalayas (Shiwalik Range / Sub-Himalayas):</strong>\nThe <strong>Shiwalik Range</strong> constitutes the outermost, southernmost, and geologically youngest chain of the Himalayan mountain system, directly abutting the Great Northern Plains:</p>\n<p><strong>Geological Origin & Lithology:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Age of Formation:</strong> Uplifted during the late <strong>Pliocene to Middle Pleistocene epochs</strong> (approx. 5 million to 1.5 million years ago).</li>\n<li><strong>Sedimentary Composition:</strong>\n  Unlike the crystalline Great Himalayas or the metamorphic Lesser Himalayas, the Shiwaliks are composed of <strong>unconsolidated riverine sediments (molasse)</strong>: sandstones, conglomerates, gravels, clays, and boulders deposited by southward-flowing rivers draining the rising inner ranges. These sediments were subsequently compressed, folded, and faulted by the ongoing northward shove of the Indian plate.</li>\n<li><strong>Physical Dimensions:</strong>\n  Extends almost continuously for $\\sim 2,400\\text{ km}$ from the Potwar Plateau in Pakistan eastward to the Brahmaputra Valley (though becoming narrow and disappearing in Sikkim/Darjeeling before reappearing in Arunachal Pradesh).\n  Altitude varies from <strong>$900\\text{ to }1,200\\text{ meters}$</strong>, with width spanning between $10\\text{ km}$ (in the east) and $50\\text{ km}$ (in Himachal and Punjab).</li>\n</ul>\n</p>\n<p><strong>The Phenomenon of Longitudinal Valleys: Duns & Duars:</strong>\nDuring the uplift of the Shiwaliks, rivers draining the Lesser Himalayas were temporarily dammed, creating long, narrow tectonic lakes. Sediments deposited in these lakes were later drained when the rivers breached the rising ridges, leaving behind flat-bottomed, fertile structural longitudinal valleys:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Duns (in the Western & Central Himalayas):</strong>\n  Extensively cultivated and settled valleys situated between the Lesser Himalayas and the Shiwalik Range.\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Dehra Dun</strong> (Uttarakhand): The largest and most famous Dun, spanning $\\sim 35\\text{ km}$ in length and $25\\text{ km}$ in width.</li>\n    <li><strong>Kotli Dun</strong> and <strong>Patli Dun</strong> (within the Corbett National Park, Uttarakhand).</li>\n    <li><strong>Pinjore Dun</strong> (Haryana/Himachal border).</li>\n    <li><strong>Kyarda Dun</strong> and <strong>Udhampur Dun</strong>.</li>\n  </ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Duars (in the Eastern Himalayas):</strong>\n  The rolling piedmont plains and gateway valleys leading into Bhutan and northeastern India (e.g., Jalpaiguri Duars, Buxa Duars), extensively developed for tea plantations.</li>\n</ul>\n</p>\n<p><strong>Regional Nomenclature of the Shiwaliks:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Jammu Hills:</strong> In Jammu and Kashmir.</li>\n<li><strong>Dhang Range and Dundwa Range:</strong> In Uttarakhand and southwestern Nepal.</li>\n<li><strong>Churia Ghat Hills:</strong> In central and southern Nepal.</li>\n<li><strong>Dafla, Miri, Abor, and Mishmi Hills:</strong> In Arunachal Pradesh.</li>\n</ul>\n</p>\n<p><strong>Environmental Vulnerability & 'Chos':</strong>\nBecause the Shiwalik strata are weakly consolidated and lack crystalline cohesion, deforestation and intense monsoonal rainfall cause catastrophic sheet and gully erosion. In the Punjab and Haryana sub-montane plains (Hoshiarpur, Ropar), seasonal, flash-flooding rainwater torrents called <strong>Chos</strong> wash away fertile topsoil and deposit coarse infertile sands across agricultural fields.</p>\n",
