            "title": "Eastern Himalayas: Sikkim & Darjeeling Himalayas",
            "summary": "Teesta River drainage basin, Kanchenjunga (8,586 m), strategic high-altitude passes to Chumbi Valley (Nathu La, Jelep La), absence of Shiwaliks replaced by Duars, and world-renowned Darjeeling tea plantation economy.",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<p><strong>The Sikkim & Darjeeling Himalayas:</strong>\nThe <strong>Sikkim and Darjeeling Himalayas</strong> forms a vital strategic enclave in the Eastern Himalayas, bounded by the Kingdom of Nepal on the west, the Kingdom of Bhutan on the east, and the Tibetan Plateau (Chumbi Valley) of China on the north:</p>\n<p><strong>Physical & Orographic Architecture:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Teesta River Drainage:</strong>\n  The entire geographical relief of Sikkim is shaped by the deep, antecedent drainage basin of the <strong>Teesta River</strong> (the lifeline of Sikkim). Originating from the <strong>Tso Lhamo Lake ($5,330\\text{ m}$)</strong> and fed by the Zemu Glacier, the Teesta carves deep, V-shaped gorges through the Greater and Lesser Himalayas before entering West Bengal and flowing into the Brahmaputra (Jamuna) in Bangladesh.</li>\n<li><strong>Kanchenjunga ($8,586\\text{ meters}$):</strong>\n  The world's 3rd highest peak towers over western Sikkim. Known in the local Lepcha language as the <em>\"Five Treasures of the High Snow\"</em> (representing gold, silver, gems, grain, and sacred books). The surrounding pristine wilderness constitutes the <strong>Khangchendzonga National Park</strong>, inscribed in 2016 as <strong>India's first Mixed UNESCO World Heritage Site</strong> (for both natural biodiversity and sacred cultural landscape).</li>\n<li><strong>The Absence of the Shiwalik Foothills:</strong>\n  In the Darjeeling and Sikkim sector, the outer Shiwalik foothills are virtually absent. The intense monsoonal rains (exceeding $3,000–4,000\\text{ mm}$) and immense horizontal tectonic compression brought the Lesser Himalayas directly into contact with the plains, creating the alluvial gateway zone known as the <strong>Duars</strong> (or Dooars).</li>\n</ul>\n</p>\n<p><strong>Strategic Passes Connecting to Tibet (Chumbi Valley):</strong>\nSikkim controls the most direct, accessible overland mountain passes bridging India and the Tibetan Plateau across the Dongkya and Jelep ranges:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>1. Nathu La ($4,310\\text{ meters}$):</strong>\n  Located in East Sikkim, $\\sim 54\\text{ km}$ east of Gangtok. It forms a primary offshoot of the ancient <strong>Old Silk Road</strong> connecting Bengal to Lhasa. Sealed following the 1962 war, it was reopened for bilateral border trade in July 2006. It also serves as a motorable overland route for Indian pilgrims undertaking the <strong>Kailash-Mansarovar Yatra</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>2. Jelep La ($4,267\\text{ meters}$):</strong>\n  Located in East Sikkim, connecting the Kalimpong and Gangtok regions through the strategic <strong>Chumbi Valley</strong> to Lhasa. Passes through the scenic Menmecho Lake.</li>\n</ul>\n</p>\n<p><strong>Economic Geography: The Darjeeling Tea Industry:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>During the 1840s, British colonial botanist Dr. Archibald Campbell established commercial experimental plantations of the Chinese tea bush (<em>Camellia sinensis</em> var. <em>sinensis</em>) in Darjeeling.</li>\n<li>The unique geo-climatic conditions—steep mountain slopes preventing waterlogging, elevations between $600\\text{ and }2,000\\text{ meters}$, cool moist mist, acidic soil, and moderate sunshine—impart a delicate muscatel flavor to <strong>Darjeeling Tea</strong>, globally acclaimed as the <strong>\"Champagne of Teas\"</strong>.</li>\n<li>Darjeeling Tea was the <strong>very first Indian product to receive a Geographical Indication (GI) tag</strong> in 2004–2005.</li>\n</ul>\n</p>\n",
