            "title": "Snowline, Glaciers & Drainage Divides of the Himalayas",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <p>The Himalayas and associated Karakoram-Hindu Kush ranges constitute the <strong>'Third Pole'</strong>, containing the largest reserve of glacial ice outside the Arctic and Antarctic. They serve as the perennial headwaters for major river systems supporting over 1.4 billion people.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. The Orographic Snowline Paradox</h4>\n            <p>The <strong>snowline</strong> is the lowest elevation where winter snowfall persists throughout the summer melting season. In the Himalayas, the snowline exhibits a counter-intuitive geographical gradient:</p>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Eastern Himalayas (Sikkim, Arunachal):</strong> Snowline is at <strong>~4,300 to 4,600 m</strong>.</li>\n                <li><strong>Western Himalayas (Kashmir, Ladakh):</strong> Snowline is at <strong>~5,500 to 5,800 m</strong>.</li>\n            </ul>\n            <p><strong>Geographical Cause:</strong> Despite the Western Himalayas lying at higher latitudes (further from the equator), the Eastern Himalayas receive abundant summer monsoon moisture from the Bay of Bengal, resulting in frequent cloud cover, heavy snowfall at high altitudes, and suppressed melting. The Western Himalayas suffer from continentality, dry air, and intense insolation, elevating the snowline.</p>\n\n            <h4>2. Major Glaciers of India</h4>\n            <p>Glacial dynamics vary significantly between the Trans-Himalayan Karakoram and the Greater Himalayas:</p>\n            <table class=\"table table-bordered\">\n                <thead>\n                    <tr><th>Glacier</th><th>Length (approx.)</th><th>Location / Mountain Range</th><th>River Sourced</th></tr>\n                </thead>\n                <tbody>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Siachen</strong></td><td>76 km</td><td>Nubra Valley, Karakoram Range</td><td>Nubra River (Shyok tributary)</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Baltoro</strong></td><td>63 km</td><td>Karakoram Range (Gilgit-Baltistan)</td><td>Shigar River (Indus tributary)</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Biafo</strong></td><td>67 km</td><td>Karakoram Range</td><td>Braldu River</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Hispar</strong></td><td>49 km</td><td>Karakoram Range</td><td>Nagar / Hunza River</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Gangotri</strong></td><td>30 km</td><td>Uttarkashi, Greater Himalayas</td><td>Bhagirathi (Ganga headstream)</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Yamunotri</strong></td><td>6 km</td><td>Bandarpunch, Greater Himalayas</td><td>Yamuna River</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Milam</strong></td><td>19 km</td><td>Pithoragarh, Kumaon Himalayas</td><td>Goriganga (Kali/Sarda)</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Pindari</strong></td><td>3 km</td><td>Bageshwar, Kumaon Himalayas</td><td>Pindar River (Alaknanda)</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Zemu</strong></td><td>26 km</td><td>Kanchenjunga, Sikkim Himalayas</td><td>Teesta River</td></tr>\n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        ",
