            "title": "Strategic Mountain Passes of Jammu & Kashmir and Ladakh",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <p>The northwestern mountain ramparts of <strong>Jammu & Kashmir and Ladakh</strong> feature some of the highest and most strategically vital mountain passes on Earth. These passes breach the Pir Panjal, Great Himalayan, Zanskar, Ladakh, and Karakoram ranges, providing lifeline conduits for civil transport and defense logistics.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Major Passes of Jammu & Kashmir</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Banihal Pass (2,832 m):</strong> Traverses the Pir Panjal Range, connecting Jammu with the Kashmir Valley. Bypassed at lower elevations by the <strong>Jawahar Tunnel (2.85 km)</strong> and the state-of-the-art <strong>Qazigund-Banihal double-tube road and rail tunnels</strong>.</li>\n                <li><strong>Pir Panjal Pass (Peer Ki Gali, 3,490 m):</strong> Historic pass on the Mughal Road linking Poonch and Rajouri with Shopian in the Kashmir Valley.</li>\n                <li><strong>Burzil Pass (4,100 m):</strong> Ancient route connecting the Kashmir Valley with the Astore Valley and Gilgit in the north; currently close to the Line of Control (LoC).</li>\n                <li><strong>Razdan Pass (3,557 m):</strong> Connects the Kashmir Valley (Bandipora) with the frontier Gurez Valley near the Kishanganga river.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Mountain Passes of Ladakh</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Zoji La (3,528 m):</strong> The vital gateway through the Great Himalayas connecting Srinagar and Baltal (Sonamarg) with Dras, Kargil, and Leh. The <strong>Zoji-la Tunnel (14.2 km)</strong> ensures all-weather connectivity.</li>\n                <li><strong>Fotu La (4,108 m) & Namika La (3,700 m):</strong> Passes across the Zaskar Range on the NH-1 highway between Kargil and Leh.</li>\n                <li><strong>Khardung La (5,359 m):</strong> Historic gateway across the Ladakh Range connecting the Indus Valley (Leh) with the strategic Shyok and Nubra valleys (leading to Siachen).</li>\n                <li><strong>Chang La (5,360 m):</strong> Connects Leh with the brackish Pangong Tso lake and Tangtse.</li>\n                <li><strong>Pensi La (4,400 m):</strong> Connects the Suru Valley (Kargil) with the isolated Zanskar Valley ('Gateway to Zanskar').</li>\n                <li><strong>Karakoram Pass (5,540 m):</strong> Ancient trans-Himalayan trade pass between Leh and the Tarim Basin (Yarkand/Kashgar in Xinjiang, China).</li>\n                <li><strong>Umling La (5,798 m / 19,024 ft):</strong> Highest motorable road pass in the world, constructed by the Border Roads Organisation (BRO) in eastern Ladakh.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
