            "title": "Mountain Passes of Himachal Pradesh & Uttarakhand",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <p>The <strong>Himachal and Uttarakhand Himalayas</strong> contain vital mountain passes that have served for millennia as corridors of trans-Himalayan barter trade, pastoral transhumance, and sacred pilgrimages to Lake Mansarovar and Mount Kailash.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Passes of Himachal Pradesh</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Rohtang Pass (3,978 m):</strong> Located in the Pir Panjal Range, connecting the lush Kullu Valley (Beas basin) with the semi-arid, Buddhist high-altitude valleys of Lahaul and Spiti (Chandra-Bhaga basin). Bypassed by the horseshoe-shaped <strong>Atal Tunnel (9.02 km)</strong> beneath Rohtang, the longest highway tunnel above 10,000 feet.</li>\n                <li><strong>Bara-lacha La (4,890 m):</strong> High mountain pass in the Zaskar Range connecting Lahaul district with Leh (Ladakh) on the Leh-Manali Highway. Acts as a water divide where the Bhaga and Chandra rivers originate.</li>\n                <li><strong>Shipki La (3,930 m):</strong> Situated in Kinnaur district on the India-China (Tibet) border. The antecedent <strong>Satluj river</strong> cuts through the Great Himalayas here to enter Indian territory.</li>\n                <li><strong>Debsa Pass (5,360 m):</strong> High glaciated mountain pass in the Greater Himalayas connecting the Parbati river valley in Kullu with the Pin Valley in Spiti.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Strategic Passes of Uttarakhand</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Lipulekh Pass (5,334 m):</strong> Located at the tri-junction of India (Pithoragarh, Kumaon), Nepal, and Tibet (China). Celebrated as the official crossing point for the annual <strong>Kailash-Mansarovar Yatra</strong> organized by the Ministry of External Affairs.</li>\n                <li><strong>Mana Pass (Dungri La, 5,545 m):</strong> High pass connecting the Badrinath valley (Alaknanda headwaters) with Tibet. Border Roads Organisation constructed one of the highest motorable roads here.</li>\n                <li><strong>Niti Pass (5,068 m):</strong> Ancient trade corridor connecting the Chamoli district (Dhauliganga basin) with the Tibetan plateau.</li>\n                <li><strong>Muling La (5,669 m):</strong> High pass north of Gangotri connecting the Bhagirathi headwaters with Tibet.</li>\n                <li><strong>Traill's Pass (5,312 m):</strong> High glaciated col connecting the Pindari and Milam valleys in Kumaon.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
