            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <p>The eastern sector of the Himalayan chain—encompassing the <strong>Sikkim and Arunachal Himalayas</strong>—is punctuated by historic high-altitude passes that pierce the border mountains into Tibet (China) and Myanmar.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Strategic Passes of Sikkim</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Nathu La (4,310 m):</strong> Situated on the Indo-China border in East Sikkim, 54 km east of Gangtok. A primary branch of the ancient Silk Road, closed after the 1962 Sino-Indian conflict and reopened in 2006 for bilateral border trade and the Kailash-Mansarovar Yatra.</li>\n                <li><strong>Jelep La (4,267 m):</strong> Located south of Nathu La, passing through the historic trade route via Kalimpong and the Chumbi Valley (Tibet). Carved by a tributary of the Teesta River.</li>\n                <li><strong>Donkia Pass (5,537 m):</strong> High pass in North Sikkim near Tso Lhamo lake (source of Teesta).</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Mountain Passes of Arunachal Pradesh</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Bomdi La (2,217 m):</strong> Connects the Tezpur plains of Assam with the Tawang district in western Arunachal.</li>\n                <li><strong>Sela Pass (4,170 m):</strong> Critical high pass between Bomdila and Tawang, now bypassed by the twin-tube Sela Tunnel.</li>\n                <li><strong>Dihang Pass (4,000 m+):</strong> Located in the northeastern corner, providing access between Arunachal and Myanmar.</li>\n                <li><strong>Diphu Pass (Dipher Pass, 4,587 m):</strong> Strategic pass located on the tri-junction of India (Arunachal Pradesh), China, and Myanmar.</li>\n                <li><strong>Pangsau Pass (1,136 m):</strong> Low mountain pass across the Patkai Bum on the India-Myanmar border, traversed by the historic World War II Ledo/Stilwell Road.</li>\n                <li><strong>Yonggyap Pass & Kepang La:</strong> River entry points and passes near the Great Syntaxial Bend of the Siang/Dihang river.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
