            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <p>The intricate topographic relief of the Himalayas is characterized by diverse valley systems shaped by tectonic downwarping, antecedent river carving, and Pleistocene glaciation.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Major Longitudinal Intermontane Basins</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Kashmir Valley:</strong> An elliptical structural depression (~135 km long, 32 km wide) situated between the Pir Panjal (southwest) and the Great Himalayas (northeast). Formed by tectonic down-faulting and impounded into an ancient Pleistocene lake (Lake Karewa), later drained by the Jhelum through the Baramulla gorge.</li>\n                <li><strong>Duns (Outer Himalayan Longitudinal Valleys):</strong> Flat-bottomed strike valleys formed between the Lesser Himalayas and the Shiwalik hogbacks. Covered in coarse alluvial fans (boulders and gravels). Examples include <strong>Dehra Dun</strong> (largest, 35 km long), <strong>Kotli Dun</strong>, <strong>Patli Dun</strong>, and <strong>Pinjore Dun</strong>.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Transverse & Fluvial Valleys</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Kangra Valley:</strong> Broad, picturesque valley in Himachal Pradesh drained by the Beas and its tributaries, nestled beneath the towering Dhauladhar range.</li>\n                <li><strong>Kullu Valley (Valley of the Gods):</strong> Transverse U-to-V-shaped valley carved by the upper Beas River, extending from Rohtang Pass down to Mandi. Famous for apple orchards.</li>\n                <li><strong>Chamba Valley:</strong> Drained by the Ravi River between the Dhauladhar and Pir Panjal ranges.</li>\n                <li><strong>Spiti Valley:</strong> Cold-desert, high-altitude rain-shadow valley drained by the Spiti River (Satluj tributary). Famous for Tibetan Buddhist monasteries (Ki, Tabo) and rich Tethyan marine fossil beds.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. Glacial & High-Altitude Biosphere Valleys</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Valley of Flowers:</strong> High-altitude Alpine valley in the Chamoli district of Uttarakhand (Pushpawati river basin), renowned for endemic alpine flora; a UNESCO World Heritage Site.</li>\n                <li><strong>Yumthang Valley:</strong> Known as the 'Sikkim Valley of Flowers', located in North Sikkim near the Lachung River, celebrated for rhododendron blooms and hot springs.</li>\n                <li><strong>Dzukou Valley:</strong> Situated on the border of Nagaland and Manipur, known for its seasonal carpets of Dzukou lilies and dwarf bamboo meadows.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
            "pointsToRemember": [
                "Himalayan valleys are categorized structurally into longitudinal strike valleys (synclinal depressions, Duns) and deep transverse antecedent river valleys.",
                "Kashmir Valley is a classic intermontane synclinal basin filled with lacustrine Karewa deposits between the Pir Panjal and Great Himalayas.",
                "Spiti and Lahaul are cold arid rain-shadow valleys, while Kangra and Kullu are lush transverse valleys drained by the Beas and Ravi.",
                "Specialized ecological valleys include the Valley of Flowers (Uttarakhand), Yumthang Valley (Sikkim), and Dzukou Valley (Nagaland-Manipur)."
            ],
            "keyNotes": [
                "Valleys in the Himalayas reflect structural synclines, fault grabs, or glacial-fluvial erosion. Longitudinal Duns (Dehradun, Kotli, Patli) lie between the Lesser Himalayas and the Shiwaliks. The Spiti valley preserves an unbroken marine fossil record from the Cambrian to Cretaceous (Tethyan sedimentary succession)."
            ],
