            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <p>Because the Himalayan orogen is an active collision zone between the Indian and Eurasian plates, it exhibits extreme <strong>geomorphic fragility, seismic instability, and slope vulnerability</strong>. Understanding these natural hazards is critical for civil administration, disaster mitigation, and infrastructure planning.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. High-Intensity Seismicity & Major Historical Earthquakes</h4>\n            <p>Under the BIS Seismic Zoning Map of India (IS 1893), the entire Himalayan belt falls in <strong>Zone IV (High Damage Risk)</strong> and <strong>Zone V (Very High Damage Risk)</strong>. Ongoing sub-crustal locking along the <strong>Main Himalayan Thrust (MHT)</strong> accumulates elastic strain energy, producing severe mega-thrust earthquakes:</p>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>1897 Great Assam Earthquake (M 8.1):</strong> Uplifted the northern scarp of the Shillong Plateau by ~10 m; reversed river courses.</li>\n                <li><strong>1905 Kangra Earthquake (M 7.8):</strong> Devastated Kangra and Dharamshala in Himachal Pradesh, causing ~20,000 casualties.</li>\n                <li><strong>1934 Bihar-Nepal Earthquake (M 8.1):</strong> Centered near the border; caused widespread liquefaction and destruction in the Gangetic plains.</li>\n                <li><strong>1950 Assam Earthquake (M 8.6):</strong> Massive earthquake on the eastern syntaxial bend; dammed the Subansiri river with massive landslides, causing catastrophic downstream deluge.</li>\n                <li><strong>2005 Kashmir Earthquake (M 7.6):</strong> Caused intense ground rupture across Muzaffarabad and Uri.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Landslides and Mass Wasting</h4>\n            <p>Factors driving high landslide susceptibility include: fractured and sheared rock strata (near MCT and MBT), steep relief, torrential monsoon rainfall saturating slope overburden, and unscientific toe-cutting during highway widening.</p>\n\n            <h4>3. Cloudbursts & GLOFs (Glacial Lake Outburst Floods)</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Cloudbursts:</strong> Localized rainfall exceeding <strong>100 mm/hour</strong> over an area of ~20–30 km², occurring when saturated convective clouds are trapped against steep mountain ridges. Triggers violent flash floods and debris flows (e.g., Leh 2010, Uttarakhand 2013).</li>\n                <li><strong>GLOFs:</strong> Accelerated glacial melting creates moraine-dammed proglacial lakes. Failure of these unstable terminal dams unleashes catastrophic torrents. Recent examples include the <strong>Kedarnath tragedy (June 2013)</strong> from Chorabari Lake, the <strong>Chamoli rock-ice avalanche (Feb 2021)</strong>, and the <strong>South Lhonak Lake GLOF in Sikkim (Oct 2023)</strong> which breached the Teesta III Chungthang dam.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
